(a) Legislative intent. It is the intent of the council in enacting this section to protect the safety of the general public against certain abusive or dangerous conduct of persons engaged in solicitation, by imposing reasonable time, manner, and place restrictions on solicitation while respecting the constitutional rights of free speech for all citizens.
(b) Definitions:
Solicit or soliciting is any request made in person on a street, sidewalk, or public place asking for an immediate donation of money or other thing of value, including the purchase of an item or service for an amount far exceeding its value, under circumstances where a reasonable person would understand that the purchase is a donation. The solicitation may be, without limitation, by the spoken, written, or printed word, by gesture, or by other means of communication.
Aggressive solicitation includes but is not limited to soliciting in any of the following manners:
(1) By intentionally or recklessly blocking the safe or free passage of a person or vehicle by any means, including unreasonably causing a pedestrian or vehicle operator to take evasive action to avoid physical contact. Acts authorized by a lawfully issued permit shall not constitute obstruction of pedestrian traffic for purposes of this section;
(2) By approaching, seeking, or following a person before, during, or after solicitation if that conduct is intended, or likely to cause, a reasonable person to:
a. Fear bodily harm to oneself or another;
b. Fear damage to or loss of property; or
c. Be intimidated.
(3) By using profane or abusive language or gestures, either during the solicitation or following refusal;
(4) By touching or causing physical contact with another person without that person's consent in the course of soliciting if the touching or physical conduct is likely to cause a reasonable person to feel restrained or intimidated; or
(5) By intentionally or recklessly using obscene, abusive, or threatening language, or gestures, toward a person being solicited.
Automated-teller machine means a device, linked to a financial institution's account records, that is able to carry out transactions, including but not limited to account transfers, deposits, cash withdrawals, balance inquiries, and mortgage and loan payments.
Automated-teller machine facility means the area comprised of one or more automated-teller machines, and any adjacent space that is made available to banking customers.
Public property means all property owned, operated, or controlled by a governmental entity within the city boundaries, including but not limited to streets, public sidewalks, tree lawns, parks, playgrounds, publicly-owned parking lots, schools, libraries, post offices, municipal transit facilities, and other public lands and buildings.
(c) Prohibited Conduct. It shall be unlawful to:
(1) Aggressively solicit within the city;
(2) Solicit on private property if the owner, tenant, or lawful occupant has asked the person not to solicit on the property, or has posted a sign clearly indicating that solicitations are not welcome on the property;
(3) Solicit within 30 feet of any automated-teller machine without the consent of the owner or other person legally in possession of the machine, provided; however, when an automated-teller machine is located within an automated-teller machine facility, the distance shall be measured from the entrance or exit of the automated-teller machine facility;
(4) Solicit within 30 feet of a line of pedestrians waiting to obtain access to a building or event;
(5) Solicit on public property within 30 feet of an entrance to a building;
(6) Solicit on public property within 30 feet of an entrance to a parking lot;
(7) Solicit on a public right-of-way designed for vehicular traffic, including medians;
(8) Solicit between the hours of 8:00 p.m. and 8:00 a.m.;
(9) Solicit in a false or misleading manner which includes but is not limited to the following:
a. Stating that the solicitor is from out of town and stranded when such is not true;
b. Stating or suggesting falsely that the solicitor is either a present or former member of the armed service indicated;
c. Wearing or displaying an indication of physical disability, when the solicitor does not suffer the disability indicated;
d. Use of any makeup or device to simulate a deformity; or
e. Stating that the solicitor is homeless, when he or she is not.
(d) Penalties. Upon the first or subsequent offenses, individuals violating subsection (c)(1) of this section shall be issued a written citation, including a summons to appear in municipal court, be punished by a fine not to exceed $1,000.00, or confinement not exceed 30 days or both. Individuals violating subsections (c)(2)-(10) of this section for the first time shall be issued a warning citation, which shall not include a summons to appear municipal court. On the second or subsequent offense, the individual shall be issued a written citation, including a summons to appear in municipal court, be punished by a fine not to exceed $1,000.00, or confinement not exceed 30 days or both.
(Ord. No. 2019-03-07, 3-7-2019)

